<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-graph-transformations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Graph Transformations</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this section, we learn how to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ol>
			<li><p>Apply graph vertical transformations.</p></li>
			<li><p>Apply graph horzontal transformations.</p></li>
			<li><p>Apply graph horizontal and vertical transformations to a function given by its graph.</p></li>
			<li><p>Apply graph horzontal transformations to standard functions (such as the absolute value function and the square root function.).</p></li>
    </ol>
	</objectives>

	<subsection>
		<title>Vertical graph transformations</title>

    <p>
      Suppose that we have a function <m>y=f(x)</m> that is introduced to us by seeing its graph.
      Based on the graph we are given, how can we see the graph of <m>y = f(x) + 3</m>?
      Because the notation of <m>y=f(x)</m> and <m>y=f(x)+3</m> are so similar, the graphs should be similar.
      Similarly, becase the notation of <m>y=f(x)</m> and <m>y=3\,f(x)</m> are so similar, the graphs should be similar.
    </p>

    <p>
      We study the case of <m>y=f(x)</m> versus <m>y=f(x)+3</m> but by looking a table of values first instead of a graph.
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the following table for <m>y=f(x)</m>, find a table for <m>y=f(x)+3</m>.
            <table>
              <tabular>
                <row header="yes">
                  <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>1</cell>
                  <cell>8</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>2</cell>
                  <cell>4</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>3</cell>
                  <cell>9</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>4</cell>
                  <cell>7</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>5</cell>
                  <cell>-10</cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
            </table>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The first row of the table tells us that <m>f(1)=8</m>. Equivalently, the point <m>(1,8)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>.
            Let's take this information to analyze what happens in the graph of <m>y=f(x)+3</m>.
            Start with <m>y=f(x)+3</m> and substitute <m>x=1</m>. 
            Then we have <m>y=f(1)+3</m> but the first term <m>f(1)</m> can be replaced with <m>8</m> based on our earlier equation, so <m>y=f(1)+3=8+3=11</m>.
            This means that the point <m>(1,11)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)+3</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            We repeat this process (a little faster) based on the next rows.
            From the table, <m>f(2)=4</m> and we have the point <m>(2,4)</m>.
            For <m>y=f(x)+3</m>, we have <m>y=f(2)+3=4+3=7</m> and the point <m>(2,7)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            From <m>f(3)=9</m> we have the point <m>(3,9)</m>. So, for <m>y=f(x)+3</m>, we have <m>y=f(3)+3=9+3=12</m> and the point <m>(3,12)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            From <m>f(4)=7</m> we have the point <m>(4,7)</m>. So, for <m>y=f(x)+3</m>, we have <m>y=f(4)+3=7+3=10</m> and the point <m>(4,10)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            From <m>f(5)=-10</m> we have the point <m>(5,-10)</m>. So, for <m>y=f(x)+3</m>, we have <m>y=f(5)+3=-10+3=-7</m> and the point <m>(5,-7)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            So, a table for <m>y=f(x)+3</m> is:
            <table>
              <tabular>
                <row header="yes">
                  <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>f(x)+3</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>1</cell>
                  <cell>11</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>2</cell>
                  <cell>7</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>3</cell>
                  <cell>12</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>4</cell>
                  <cell>10</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>5</cell>
                  <cell>-7</cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
            </table>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      We see from this example the following connection between the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and the graph of <m>y=f(x)+3</m>:
      if the point <m>(a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the point <m>(a,b+3)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)+3</m>.
      Note that we only added <m>3</m> to the <m>y</m>-coordinate, not the <m>x</m>-coordinate:
      geometrically, that means in the new graph, a point is drawn <m>3</m> units higher.
      We can generalize this:
      <principle xml:id="principle-transform-up">
        <title>Vertical shift up</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In comparing the "old" graph <m>y=f(x)</m> to the "new" graph <m>y=f(x)+k</m>,
            if the point <m>(a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the point <m>(a,b+k)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)+k</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            In other words, the graph of <m>y=f(x)+k</m> is obtained by shifting the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> up <m>k</m> units.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      When we apply this movement of vertically shifting up (which is one of several graph transformations that we'll see), the axes do not move, but the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> shifts up (relative to thè axes) by <m>k</m> units to obtain the graph of <m>y=f(x)+k</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let's exercise what <xref ref="principle-transform-up"/> says by looking at several different kinds of examples.
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>(31,20)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, find a point on the graph of <m>y=f(x)+7</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The point <m>(31,27)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)+7</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      Note that we only added <m>7</m> to the <m>y</m>-coordinate, and not to the <m>x</m>-coordinate.
      Geometrically, this means that the point is drawn <m>7</m> units higher in the new graph.
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>(83,25)</m> and <m>(100,30)</m> and <m>(120,7)</m> are points on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, find three points on the graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The points <m>(83,27)</m> and <m>(100,32)</m> and <m>(120,9)</m>are on the graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      Note that we were given three points in the old graph, and each point led us to a point in the new graph.
      It may be the case that the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> has more points, but we weren't given any additional information, so we could only report on three points of <m>y=f(x)+2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The last example intentionally asked about what happens to three points. We can apply that same process (involving multiple points, each moving up by the same amount) directly from a graph, as in: directly from the picture.
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,2) -- (0,1) -- (2,3) -- (4,-1) -- (6,4);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,4) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            To sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m>, take each point on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and move it up by 2 units. 
            The overall shape of the graph remains the same, but every point is shifted vertically upward by 2 units.
          </p>
          <p>
            In the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, we have the points <m>(-6,2)</m> and <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>(2,3)</m> and <m>(4,-1)</m> and <m>(6,4)</m>.
            Note that even though <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>(2,3)</m> and <m>(4,-1)</m> are not drawn in thick, these are points in the graph.
            In addition, we have all the points in between <m>(-6,2)</m> and <m>(0,1)</m>, though these are not drawn in thick.            
          </p>
          <p>
            Because <m>(-6,2)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, we know that <m>(-6,4)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m>.
            Because <m>(0,1)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, we know that <m>(0,3)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m>.
            We can keep going in this way, and we find that the points <m>(2,5)</m> and <m>(4,1)</m> and <m>(6,6)</m> are on the graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            To draw a graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m>, we can start by plotting the points <m>(-6,4)</m> and <m>(0,3)</m> and <m>(2,5)</m> and <m>(4,1)</m> and <m>(6,6)</m>, 
            and then connect them in a "connect the dots" style. So, the graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m> is:
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,4) -- (0,3) -- (2,5) -- (4,1) -- (6,6);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,4) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,6) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m></caption>
            </figure>
            Notice that the graph of <m>y=f(x)+2</m> is the same shape as the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, but it is shifted up by 2 units.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x)+1</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,-3) -- (-5,-4) -- (-3,1) -- (1,-1) -- (4,0) -- (6,4);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-3) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,4) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> connects the points <m>(-6,-3)</m> and <m>(-5,-4)</m> and <m>(-3,1)</m> and <m>(1,-1)</m> and <m>(4,0)</m> and <m>(6,4)</m>.
            To sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x)+1</m>, we take each of these points and move them up by 1 unit.
            So the graph of <m>y=f(x)+1</m> connects the points <m>(-6,-2)</m> and <m>(-5,-3)</m> and <m>(-3,2)</m> and <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(4,1)</m> and <m>(6,5)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,-2) -- (-5,-3) -- (-3,2) -- (1,0) -- (4,1) -- (6,5);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,5) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)+1</m></caption>
            </figure>
            The graph of <m>y=f(x)+1</m> is the same shape as the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, but it is shifted up by 1 unit.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      Now we compare the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> to the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m>.
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>(9,10)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, find a point on the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Since <m>(9,10)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, we know <m>f(9)=10</m>.
            We examine <m>y=2\,f(x)</m> and substitute <m>x=9</m> to get <m>y=2\,f(9)</m>.
            We can replace <m>f(9)</m> with <m>10</m> to get <m>y=2\cdot 10=20</m>.
            So the point <m>(9,20)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      Note that we only multiplied the <m>y</m>-coordinate by <m>2</m>, and but did not alter the <m>x</m>-coordinate.
      Geometrically, this means that the point is drawn <m>2</m> times as high in the new graph.
      <principle xml:id="principle-transform-vertical-stretch">
        <title>Vertical stretch</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In comparing the "old" graph <m>y=f(x)</m> to the "new" graph <m>y=af(x)</m>,
            if the point <m>(a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the point <m>(a,kb)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=k\,f(x)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            In other words, the graph of <m>y=k\,f(x)</m> is obtained by stretching the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> vertically by a factor of <m>k</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>

    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,2) -- (0,1) -- (2,3) -- (4,-1) -- (6,4);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,4) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </statement>      
        <answer>
          <p>
            To sketch the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m>, we take each point on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and double its <m>y</m>-coordinate.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,4) -- (0,2) -- (2,6) -- (4,-2) -- (6,8);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,4) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
            Note in this example, thge point <m>(4,-1)</m> on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> led to learning about the point <m>(4,-2)</m> on the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m>.
            That is, it is not fair to say that "all points moved up". Instead, everything was stretch <em>away</em> from the <m>x</m>-axis by a factor of <m>2</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      Since <m>y=k\,f(x)</m> is a vertical stretch, we can also consider <m>y=\frac{1}{k}\,f(x)</m> as a vertical compression.
      <principle xml:id="principle-transform-vertical-compression">
        <title>Vertical compression</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In comparing the "old" graph <m>y=f(x)</m> to the "new" graph <m>y=af(x)</m>,
            if the point <m>(a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the point <m>(a,\frac{1}{k}b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=\frac{1}{k}\,f(x)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            In other words, the graph of <m>y=\frac{1}{k}\,f(x)</m> is obtained by compressing the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> vertically by a factor of <m>k</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>

    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=\frac{1}{2}\,f(x)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,4) -- (0,-2) -- (2,6) -- (4,-4) -- (6,-4);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,4) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-4) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            To sketch the graph of <m>y=\frac{1}{2}\,f(x)</m>, we take each point on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and halve its <m>y</m>-coordinate.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,2) -- (0,-1) -- (2,3) -- (4,-2) -- (6,-2);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=\frac{1}{2}\,f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
            We took each point in <m>y=f(x)</m> and halved its <m>y</m>-coordinate to get the corresponding point in <m>y=\frac{1}{2}\,f(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>      
    </p>

    <p>
      Finally, we discuss the connection between the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and the graph of <m>y=-f(x)</m>.
      <principle xml:id="principle-transform-vertical-flip">
        <title>Vertical flip</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In comparing the "old" graph <m>y=f(x)</m> to the "new" graph <m>y=-f(x)</m>,
            if the point <m>(a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the point <m>(a,-b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=-f(x)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            In other words, the graph of <m>y=-f(x)</m> is obtained by flipping the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> vertically about the <m>x</m>-axis -- in other words, flipping the graph to be upside down.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=-f(x)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,3) -- (0,-2) -- (2,-4) -- (4,-4) -- (6,4);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,3) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,4) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,-3) -- (0,2) -- (2,4) -- (4,4) -- (6,-4);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-3) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-4) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=-f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    
    <p>
      Lastly, we need to discuss what happens when there are multiple vertical transformations.
      In short, we follow the order of operations: multiplication/division before addition/subtraction.
      So, given <m>y=f(x)</m> to graph <m>y=2\,f(x)+1</m>:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Take the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and stretch it vertically by a factor of <m>2</m> to get the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Then, take the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m> and shift it up by <m>1</m> to get the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)+1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)+1</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,-2) -- (0,1) -- (2,0) -- (4,-3) -- (6,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            First, we take the given graph and stretch it vertically by a factor of <m>2</m> to get the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,-4) -- (0,2) -- (2,0) -- (4,-6) -- (6,-2);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-4) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                     \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
            Then, we take the resulting graph and shift it up by <m>1</m> to get the graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)+1</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,-3) -- (0,3) -- (2,1) -- (4,-5) -- (6,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-3) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=2\,f(x)+1</m></caption>
            </figure>

          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>      
    </p>  
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-horizontal-transformations">
    <title>Horizontal graph transformations</title>

    <p>
      How do we compare the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> to the graph of <m>y=f(x+3)</m>?
      As a matter of experiment, say that we know that the point <m>(5,11)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>.
      Then, what point would be learn of on the graph of <m>y=f(x+3)</m>?
      Since <m>(5,11)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, we know that <m>f(5)=11</m>.
      We examine <m>y=f(x+3)</m> and substitute <m>x=2</m> to get <m>y=f(2+3)</m> which simplifies to <m>y=f(5)</m>, so <m>y=11</m>.
      So the point <m>(2,11)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x+3)</m>.
      In other words, the point <m>(5,11)</m> on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> led to the point <m>(2,11)</m> on the graph of <m>y=f(x+3)</m>.
      Geometrically, this means that the point is drawn <m>3</m> units to the left in the new graph. 
      <principle xml:id="principle-shift-left">
        <title>Horizontal shift left</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In comparing the "old" graph <m>y=f(x)</m> to the "new" graph <m>y=f(x+k)</m>,
            if the point <m>(a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the point <m>(a-k,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x+k)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            In other words, the graph of <m>y=f(x+k)</m> is obtained by shifting the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> horizontally to the left by <m>k</m> units.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      <principle xml:id="principle-shift-right">
        <title>Horizontal shift right</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In comparing the "old" graph <m>y=f(x)</m> to the "new" graph <m>y=f(x-k)</m>,
            if the point <m>(a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the point <m>(a+k,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x-k)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            In other words, the graph of <m>y=f(x-k)</m> is obtained by shifting the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> horizontally to the right by <m>k</m> units.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x+2)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-3,-2) -- (0,1) -- (2,0) -- (3,-3) -- (4,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-3,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (4,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            To sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x+2)</m>, we take each point on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and move it left by <m>2</m> units.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-5,-2) -- (-2,1) -- (0,0) -- (1,-3) -- (2,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-5,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (2,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x+2)</m></caption>
            </figure>
            The graph of <m>y=f(x+2)</m> is the same shape as the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, but it is shifted left by 2 units.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>


    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x-2)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-3,-2) -- (0,1) -- (2,0) -- (3,-3) -- (4,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-3,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (4,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            To sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x-2)</m>, we take each point on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and move it right by <m>2</m> units.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-1,-2) -- (2,1) -- (4,0) -- (5,-3) -- (6,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-1,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x-2)</m></caption>
            </figure>
            The graph of <m>y=f(x-2)</m> is the same shape as the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, but it is shifted right by 2 units.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      How do we compare the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> to the graph of <m>y=f(2x)</m>?
      As a matter of experiment, say that we know that the point <m>(6,12)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>.
      Then, what point would be learn of on the graph of <m>y=f(2x)</m>?
      Since <m>(6,12)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, we know that <m>f(6)=12</m>.
      We examine <m>y=f(2x)</m> and substitute <m>x=3</m> to get <m>y=f(2 \cdot 3)</m> which simplifies to <m>y=f(6)</m>, so <m>y=12</m>.
      So the point <m>(3,12)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(2x)</m>.
      In other words, the point <m>(6,12)</m> on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> led to the point <m>(3,12)</m> on the graph of <m>y=f(2x)</m>.
      Geometrically, this means that the point is horizontally compressed by a factor of <m>2</m>.
      <principle xml:id="principle-horizontal-compression">
        <title>Horizontal compression</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In comparing the "old" graph <m>y=f(x)</m> to the "new" graph <m>y=f(kx)</m>,
            if the point <m>(a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the point <m>(\frac{a}{k}a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(kx)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            In other words, the graph of <m>y=f(kx)</m> is obtained by compressing the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> horizontally by a factor of <m>k</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      <principle xml:id="principle-horizontal-stretch">
        <title>Horizontal stretch</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In comparing the "old" graph <m>y=f(x)</m> to the "new" graph <m>y=f(\frac1k x)</m>,
            if the point <m>(a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the point <m>(k a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(\frac1k x)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            In other words, the graph of <m>y=f(\frac1k x)</m> is obtained by stretching the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> horizontally by a factor of <m>k</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>      
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=f(2x)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,-2) -- (-4,1) -- (-2,0) -- (2,-3) -- (6,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </statement>      
        <answer>
          <p>
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-3,-2) -- (-2,1) -- (-1,0) -- (1,-3) -- (3,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(2x)</m></caption>
            </figure>            
            The graph is obtained from the given graph by compressing horizontally by a factor of <m>2</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>



    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=f(\frac12x)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-3,-2) -- (-2,1) -- (1,-1) -- (2,-3) -- (3,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-3,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (3,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </statement>      
        <answer>
          <p>
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,-2) -- (-4,1) -- (2,-1) -- (4,-3) -- (6,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(\frac12x)</m></caption>
            </figure>            
            The graph is obtained from the given graph by stretching horizontally by a factor of <m>2</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Combining horizontal and vertical transformations</title>

    <p>
      Whenever we have both horizontal and vertical transformations, we perform all horizontal transformations first.
      Once all horizontal transformations are complete, we then perform all vertical transformations.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, given <m>y=f(x+2)+3</m>, we first shift the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> left by <m>2</m> units to get the graph of <m>y=f(x+2)</m>.
      Then, we take the graph of <m>y=f(x+2)</m> and shift it up by <m>3</m> units to get the graph of <m>y=f(x+2)+3</m>.
    </p>

     <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x+2)+3</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-3,-2) -- (-2,1) -- (1,-1) -- (2,-3) -- (3,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-3,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (3,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </statement>         
        <answer>
          <p>
            We first take the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and shift it left by <m>2</m> units to get the graph of <m>y=f(x+2)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-5,-2) -- (-4,1) -- (-1,-1) -- (0,-3) -- (1,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-5,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (1,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x+2)</m></caption>
            </figure>
            Then, we take the graph of <m>y=f(x+2)</m> and shift it up by <m>3</m> units to get the graph of <m>y=f(x+2)+3</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-5,1) -- (-4,4) -- (-1,2) -- (0,0) -- (1,2);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x+2)+3</m></caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

     <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x-1)+2</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-3,-2) -- (-2,1) -- (1,-1) -- (2,-3) -- (3,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-3,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (3,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </statement>       
        <answer>
          <p>
            We take the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and shift it right by <m>1</m> unit to get the graph of <m>y=f(x-1)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-2,-2) -- (-1,1) -- (2,-1) -- (3,-3) -- (4,-1);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x-1)</m></caption>
            </figure>
            Then, we take the graph of <m>y=f(x-1)</m> and shift it up by <m>2</m> units to get the graph of <m>y=f(x-1)+2</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-2,0) -- (-1,3) -- (2,1) -- (3,-1) -- (4,1);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x-1)+2</m></caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>


     <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, sketch the graph of <m>y=f(\frac12x)-1</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-3,-2) -- (-2,1) -- (1,-1) -- (2,-3) -- (3,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-3,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (3,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </statement>       

        <answer>
          <p>
            We take the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> and stretch it horizontally by a factor of <m>2</m> to get the graph of <m>y=f(\frac12x)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,-2) -- (-4,1) -- (2,-1) -- (4,-3) -- (6,-1);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-1) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(\frac12x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
            Then, we take the graph of <m>y=f(\frac12x)</m> and shift it down by <m>1</m> unit to get the graph of <m>y=f(\frac12x)-1</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-6,-6) grid (6,6);
                      \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                      \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                      \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-6,-3) -- (-4,0) -- (2,-2) -- (4,-4) -- (6,-2);
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (-6,-3) circle [radius=0.1];
                      \draw[blue, ultra thick, fill=blue] (6,-2) circle [radius=0.1];
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(\frac12x)-1</m></caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

  </subsection>



</section>